Subject: [PATCH] x86-64: relax TLS to the %gs carrier instead of refusing

Patch 0001 refused TLSGD, TLSLD, GOTTPOFF and the TLSDESC relocations,
because ld relaxes those sequences in place and writes mov %fs:0x0,%rax
itself (spike/ld-tls-relaxation/), and the carrier DR-0003 had chosen
needed three instructions where the psABI leaves room for two.

DR-0101 moved the thread pointer to TlsSlots[63] of the TEB, %gs:0x1678, one
load. mov %gs:0x1678,%rax is 65 48 8b 04 25 78 16 00 00, the same nine bytes
as mov %fs:0x0,%rax (64 48 8b 04 25 00 00 00 00): a segment prefix, REX.W,
the opcode, a SIB-absolute ModRM and a disp32. So the room the psABI reserved
for the local-exec form holds the carrier's fetch too, and the relaxations
can write it. This patch removes the refusal and changes every fetch the
relaxations write, in the eleven byte strings and the comments that describe
them; nothing else about the rewrites changes.

The refusal was also broader than its reason. GOTTPOFF's relaxation writes an
immediate and no segment prefix at all, and the %fs:(%rax) in the spike's
initial-exec input was the assembler's, not ld's; refusing it was what stopped
glibc's own initial-exec code, and every static link against libc.a, from
linking. The compiler no longer emits that form (toolchain/gcc/patches/0002)
and toolchain/glibc/check-no-syscall refuses it in a finished object, which
is where an assembler-written %fs is caught.

The x32 form (movl %fs:0,%eax, four bytes plus disp32) changes the same way.
x32 is not built for this target; the change keeps the two forms saying the
same thing.

Against binutils 2.42, pinned in ../binutils.pin, over patch 0001.

--- a/bfd/elf64-x86-64.c
+++ b/bfd/elf64-x86-64.c
@@ -2151,61 +2151,6 @@
